{
 "question": "is the shipped-model provenance chain tamper-EVIDENT end to end, and can the keyless half be verified without the HMAC key? §CX.5 claimed the chain cannot resist tampering because the manifest sits beside the files -- is that reasoning complete?",
 "generated_at_local": "2026-09-19T14:58:10",
 "correction_to_CX5": {
  "what_CX5_said": "not citable: 'this chain resists tampering' -- reason given was that checksums.sha256 sits beside the files it protects with the same permissions",
  "why_that_reason_is_incomplete": "the reasoning stops one link too early. The evaluator ALSO checks _evidence_id(payload) == evidence_id (selection_evidence.py:145), a KEYLESS sha256 over the canonical payload, and model_bundle_digest lives inside that payload. So editing any bundle file forces editing the manifest, which changes the bundle digest, which forces editing the payload, which breaks the keyless content-hash check. Modification is DETECTED.",
  "corrected_claim": "the chain is tamper-EVIDENT, not tamper-PROOF: any modification is detected by the evaluator, and producing an UNDETECTED modification additionally requires the HMAC key (QRV2_SELECTION_EVIDENCE_HMAC_KEY), which this pack does not read",
  "what_remains_unproven": "the HMAC signature itself is not verified (no key), so an attacker holding the key could forge an entirely new consistent record that passes every check; and that these records were signed by the trusted pre-TEST selection service cannot be shown without the key"
 },
 "the_five_links": {
  "L1": "report.json names the fold's model_selection_id",
  "L2": "evidence_id is the directory name <eid>.json AND the record's own evidence_id field",
  "L3": "evidence_id == sha256(canonical_json(payload)) -- KEYLESS content binding",
  "L4": "payload.model_bundle_digest == sha256(checksums.sha256 bytes)",
  "L5": "every bundle file's sha256 == its line in checksums.sha256",
  "which_are_new_here": [
   "L1",
   "L2",
   "L3"
  ],
  "which_restate_CX": [
   "L4",
   "L5"
  ]
 },
